WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.568
Distributed systems operate across multiple
networked computers where components

2
00:00:07.568 --> 00:00:10.910
inevitably experience partial failure.

3
00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:18.527
When a node slows down, it becomes
indistinguishable from a crashed server,

4
00:00:18.527 --> 00:00:24.875
forcing designers to make explicit assumptions
about timing and communication.

5
00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:33.607
This uncertainty means we cannot rely on
simple timeouts to detect outages.

6
00:00:33.607 --> 00:00:42.202
Instead, we must design protocols that
tolerate delayed messages, duplicated

7
00:00:42.202 --> 00:00:46.500
transmissions, and unpredictable network
schedules.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:54.671
Understanding these constraints is the first
step toward building reliable consensus

9
00:00:54.671 --> 00:01:02.100
mechanisms that survive real-world network
chaos without assuming perfect synchronization

10
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.071
or instantaneous failure detection.

11
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:12.190
To guarantee consistency across multiple
nodes, we replicate state machines that

12
00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:17.267
process identical operations in the exact same
order.

13
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:26.095
Each server maintains a local copy of the
system state, but the critical challenge is

14
00:01:26.095 --> 00:01:31.981
ensuring they apply operations in identical
sequences despite network delays.

15
00:01:31.981 --> 00:01:40.481
If servers apply commands differently, the
system diverges into conflicting states.

16
00:01:40.481 --> 00:01:49.348
By forcing all nodes to execute the same
sequence of operations, we eliminate state

17
00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:49.981
divergence.

18
00:01:49.981 --> 00:01:56.305
This approach transforms distributed
coordination into a single-threaded execution

19
00:01:56.305 --> 00:02:05.439
problem, where correctness depends entirely on
the order of applied operations rather than

20
00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:08.249
complex distributed state synchronization.

21
00:02:08.402 --> 00:02:16.920
To enforce identical execution order, we use a
replicated log where every command is appended

22
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.191
as a sequential entry.

23
00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:28.349
Each log entry contains a unique term number
and a sequence index, creating a total order

24
00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:30.066
across all servers.

25
00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:37.850
When a client submits a request, the leader
appends it to its log before acknowledging

26
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:38.369
success.

27
00:02:38.369 --> 00:02:45.798
Followers replicate the entry by appending it
to their own logs at the same index.

28
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:54.275
This log structure ensures that every server
records the exact same operations in the exact

29
00:02:54.275 --> 00:02:55.405
same order.

30
00:02:55.405 --> 00:03:04.699
The log becomes the single source of truth for
system state, preventing conflicting histories

31
00:03:04.699 --> 00:03:10.673
and enabling reliable recovery after crashes
or network partitions.

32
00:03:10.826 --> 00:03:17.579
A leader is elected to coordinate log
replication and client requests.

33
00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:25.651
Servers start as followers and transition to
candidates when they miss heartbeats.

34
00:03:25.651 --> 00:03:34.347
Randomized election timeouts prevent split
votes by staggering candidate timeouts.

35
00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:43.102
When a candidate requests votes, it increments
a term number and includes its log's last

36
00:03:43.102 --> 00:03:43.686
entry.

37
00:03:43.686 --> 00:03:50.883
Followers grant votes only if the candidate's
log is at least as up-to-date.

38
00:03:50.883 --> 00:03:58.133
Once a candidate receives votes from a
majority, it becomes leader for that term.

39
00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:06.189
Terms provide a logical clock that prevents
stale leaders from interfering with newer

40
00:04:06.189 --> 00:04:14.245
elections, ensuring only the most recent
leader can commit entries and maintain system

41
00:04:14.245 --> 00:04:14.865
progress.

42
00:04:15.002 --> 00:04:24.130
Progress requires a majority quorum of nodes
to agree on log entries and leadership.

43
00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:33.330
A majority ensures that any two quorums share
at least one node, preventing conflicting

44
00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:33.987
decisions.

45
00:04:33.987 --> 00:04:41.849
If a network partition isolates a minority,
the majority partition continues operating

46
00:04:41.849 --> 00:04:44.469
while the minority stalls.

47
00:04:44.469 --> 00:04:51.434
This design prioritizes safety over
availability during splits.

48
00:04:51.434 --> 00:04:57.719
The quorum requirement guarantees that
committed entries are durable across restarts

49
00:04:57.719 --> 00:05:02.862
and that only one leader can exist per term.

50
00:05:02.862 --> 00:05:11.519
Without a majority, the system cannot safely
commit operations or elect a new leader,

51
00:05:11.519 --> 00:05:17.702
ensuring that conflicting histories are never
permitted during network failures.

52
00:05:17.858 --> 00:05:25.808
The leader replicates log entries to followers
by sending AppendEntries RPCs.

53
00:05:25.808 --> 00:05:35.270
Each RPC includes the leader's term, the index
of the entry to replicate, and the entry

54
00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:35.861
itself.

55
00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:41.611
Followers respond with success or failure
based on log matching.

56
00:05:41.611 --> 00:05:49.950
If a follower's log conflicts, the leader
backs off and retries with a lower index.

57
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:56.843
This process ensures all followers eventually
match the leader's log.

58
00:05:56.843 --> 00:06:05.585
The log-matching property guarantees that if
two logs contain an entry with the same index

59
00:06:05.585 --> 00:06:09.665
and term, all preceding entries are identical.

60
00:06:09.665 --> 00:06:18.255
This property enables reliable recovery and
prevents divergent states during message loss

61
00:06:18.255 --> 00:06:26.129
or server restarts, maintaining a consistent
replicated log across the cluster.

62
00:06:26.282 --> 00:06:33.836
An entry becomes committed when it is
replicated to a majority of servers and the

63
00:06:33.836 --> 00:06:36.857
leader's term matches the entry's term.

64
00:06:36.857 --> 00:06:44.752
This rule ensures that only entries from the
current leader are committed, preventing stale

65
00:06:44.752 --> 00:06:47.571
leaders from overwriting newer data.

66
00:06:47.571 --> 00:06:53.785
Once committed, the entry is applied to the
state machine in order.

67
00:06:53.785 --> 00:07:01.817
The commit rule relies on the log-matching
property to guarantee that all servers

68
00:07:01.817 --> 00:07:06.142
applying the entry share identical preceding
history.

69
00:07:06.142 --> 00:07:14.362
This prevents conflicting state updates and
ensures that committed operations are durable

70
00:07:14.362 --> 00:07:15.732
across crashes.

71
00:07:15.732 --> 00:07:23.807
The term-based commitment rule also handles
leader changes gracefully, ensuring that only

72
00:07:23.807 --> 00:07:28.517
the most recent leader's entries are
finalized.

73
00:07:28.658 --> 00:07:36.374
Safety ensures that committed entries are
never lost or overwritten, while liveness

74
00:07:36.374 --> 00:07:40.875
guarantees that the system continues making
progress.

75
00:07:40.875 --> 00:07:49.557
Raft prioritizes safety by refusing to commit
entries from stale leaders and requiring

76
00:07:49.557 --> 00:07:50.893
majority agreement.

77
00:07:50.893 --> 00:07:58.429
Liveness is maintained through randomized
election timeouts and leader heartbeats.

78
00:07:58.429 --> 00:08:06.358
When a leader fails, followers detect the
absence of heartbeats and trigger elections.

79
00:08:06.358 --> 00:08:14.750
If a majority is available, a new leader is
elected and replication resumes.

80
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:21.804
If a minority is isolated, the system stalls
but remains safe.

81
00:08:21.804 --> 00:08:30.313
This trade-off ensures that the system never
commits conflicting histories, even at the

82
00:08:30.313 --> 00:08:36.858
cost of temporary unavailability during
network partitions or leader failures.

83
00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:44.986
Clients must retry operations when they
receive no response, but retries can cause

84
00:08:44.986 --> 00:08:49.281
duplicate commands if the original request
succeeded.

85
00:08:49.281 --> 00:08:56.549
To handle this, clients include a unique
request ID with each operation.

86
00:08:56.549 --> 00:09:04.888
The server checks if the ID has already been
processed and returns the cached result if so.

87
00:09:04.888 --> 00:09:11.835
This idempotency mechanism prevents duplicate
state updates.

88
00:09:11.835 --> 00:09:19.638
Retries are essential for availability during
network delays or leader elections.

89
00:09:19.638 --> 00:09:28.539
By combining unique request IDs with
server-side deduplication, clients can safely

90
00:09:28.539 --> 00:09:32.585
retry without risking conflicting operations.

91
00:09:32.585 --> 00:09:40.581
This approach ensures that clients can recover
from transient failures while maintaining

92
00:09:40.581 --> 00:09:45.245
system consistency and avoiding duplicate
state mutations.

93
00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:53.895
Tracing a command through a three-node cluster
reveals how Raft coordinates consensus.

94
00:09:53.895 --> 00:10:02.236
A client sends a request to the leader, which
appends it to its log and replicates it to

95
00:10:02.236 --> 00:10:02.699
followers.

96
00:10:02.699 --> 00:10:10.547
Once a majority acknowledges the entry, the
leader commits it and applies it to its state

97
00:10:10.547 --> 00:10:11.038
machine.

98
00:10:11.038 --> 00:10:16.592
Followers apply the entry in order after
replication.

99
00:10:16.592 --> 00:10:24.502
If the leader fails, followers detect the
missing heartbeats and trigger elections.

100
00:10:24.502 --> 00:10:31.770
A new leader is elected, and replication
resumes from the last committed entry.

101
00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:40.971
This trace demonstrates how partial failures,
log replication, and majority quorums work

102
00:10:40.971 --> 00:10:44.038
together to maintain consistency.

103
00:10:44.038 --> 00:10:52.888
The process ensures that every command is
processed exactly once, even during network

104
00:10:52.888 --> 00:11:00.377
delays or node crashes, providing a reliable
foundation for distributed applications.
